(     Extraordinarius at the University of Jena, and in 1807 he gave the) Tj T*
(     world the first of his great works, the "Phenomenology." It was not) Tj T*
(     until 1816 that Hegel's growing fame as a writer secured for him a) Tj T*
(     professorship at Heidelberg, but, after two years, he exchanged it) Tj T*
(     for one at Berlin, where he remained until his death on November) Tj T*
(     14, 1831. On October 22, 1818, he began his famous lectures. "Our) Tj T*
(     business and vocation," he remarked to his listeners, "is to) Tj T*
(     cherish the philosophical development of the substantial foundation) Tj T*
(     which has renewed its youth and increased its strength." Although) Tj T*
(     the lectures on the "Philosophy of History" and on the "Philosophy) Tj T*
(     of Religion" \(Vol. XIII\) were delivered during this period, they) Tj T*
(     were not published until a year after his death, when his collected) Tj T*
(     works were issued.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_I.--In the East Began History_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Universal or world-history travels from east to west, for Europe is) Tj T*
(absolutely the end of history, Asia the beginning. The history of the) Tj T*
(world has an east in an absolute sense, for, although the earth forms a) Tj T*
(sphere, history describes no orbit round it, but has, on the contrary, a) Tj T*
(determinate orient--_viz._, Asia. Here rises the outward visible sun,) Tj T*
(and in the west it sinks down; here also rises the sun of) Tj T*
(self-consciousness. The history of the world is a discipline of the) Tj T*
(uncontrolled natural will, bringing it into obedience to a universal) Tj T*
(principle and conferring a subjective freedom. The East knew, and to) Tj T*
(this day knows, freedom only for one; the Greek and Roman world knew) Tj T*
(that some are free; the German world knows that all are free. The first) Tj T*
(political form, therefore, that we see in history is despotism; the) Tj T*
(second democracy and aristocracy; and the third monarchy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first phase--that with which we have to begin--is the East.) Tj T*
(Unreflected consciousness--substantial, objective, spiritual) Tj T*
(existence--forms the basis; to which the subjective will first sustains) Tj T*
(a relation in the form of faith, confidence, obedience. In the political) Tj T*
(life of the East we find realised national freedom, developing itself) Tj T*
(without advancing to subjective freedom. It is the childhood of history.) Tj T*
(In the gorgeous edifices of the Oriental empires we find all national) Tj T*
(ordinances and arrangements, but in such a way that individuals remain) Tj T*
(as mere accidents. These revolve round a centre, round the sovereign,) Tj T*
(who as patriarch stands \(not as despot, in the sense of the Roman) Tj T*
(imperial constitution\) at the head. For he has to enforce the moral and) Tj T*
(substantial; he has to uphold those essential ordinances which are) Tj T*
(already established; so that what among us belongs entirely to) Tj T*
(subjective freedom, here proceeds from the entire and general body of) Tj T*
(the state.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The glory of the Oriental conception is the one individual as the) Tj T*
(substantial being to which all belongs, so that no other individual has) Tj T*
(a separate existence, or mirrors himself in his subjective freedom. All) Tj T*
(the riches of imagination and nature are appropriated to that dominant) Tj T*
(existence in which subjective freedom is essentially merged; the latter) Tj T*
ET
