(looks for its dignity not in itself but in the absolute object. All the) Tj T*
(elements of a complete state--even subjectivity--may be found there, but) Tj T*
(not yet harmonised with the grand substantial being. For outside the one) Tj T*
(power--before which nothing can maintain an independent existence--there) Tj T*
(is only revolting caprice, which, beyond the limits of the central) Tj T*
(power, moves at will without purpose or result.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Accordingly we find the wild herds breaking out from the upland,) Tj T*
(falling upon the countries in question and laying them waste, or) Tj T*
(settling down in them and giving up their wild life; but in all cases) Tj T*
(lost resultlessly in the central substance.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This phase of substantiality, since it has not taken up its antithesis) Tj T*
(into itself and overcome it, directly divides itself into two elements.) Tj T*
(On the one side we see duration, stability--empires belonging, as it) Tj T*
(were, to mere Space \(as distinguished from Time\); unhistorical history,) Tj T*
(as, for example, in China, the state based on the family relation. Yet) Tj T*
(the states in question, without undergoing any change in themselves, or) Tj T*
(in the principle of their existence, are constantly changing their) Tj T*
(opinion towards each other. They are in ceaseless conflict, which brings) Tj T*
(on rapid destruction. The opposing principle of individuality enters) Tj T*
(into these conflicting relations; but it is itself as yet only) Tj T*
(unconscious, merely natural universality--light which is not yet the) Tj T*
(light of the personal soul. This history, too, is for the most part) Tj T*
(really unhistorical, for it is only the repetition of the same majestic) Tj T*
(ruin.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The new element which, in the shape of bravery, prowess, magnanimity,) Tj T*
(occupies the place of the previous despotic pomp goes through the same) Tj T*
(cycle of decline and subsidence. And this subsidence, therefore, is not) Tj T*
(really such; for through all this restless change no advance has been) Tj T*
(made. History passes at this point--and only outwardly, that is, without) Tj T*
(connection with the previous phase--to Central Asia. To carry on the) Tj T*
(comparison with the individual man, this would be the boyhood of) Tj T*
(history, no longer manifesting the repose and trustfulness of the child,) Tj T*
(but boisterous and turbulent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_II.--Greece, Rome and Christianity_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Greek world may, then, be compared to the season of adolescence, for) Tj T*
(here we have individualities shaping themselves. This is the second) Tj T*
(main principle in human history. Morality is, as in Asia, a principle,) Tj T*
(but it is morality impressed on individuality, and consequently denoting) Tj T*
(the free volition of individuals. Here, then, is the union of the moral) Tj T*
(with the subjective will, or the kingdom of beautiful freedom, for the) Tj T*
(idea is united with a plastic form. It is not yet regarded abstractly,) Tj T*
(but intimately bound up with the real, as in a beautiful work of art;) Tj T*
(the sensible bears the stamp and expression of the spiritual. The) Tj T*
(kingdom is consequently true harmony; it is a world of the most charming) Tj T*
(but perishable, or quickly passing, bloom; it is the natural,) Tj T*
(unreflecting observance of what is becoming--not yet true morality. The) Tj T*
ET
