() Tj T*
(It was through the Christian religion that the absolute idea of God, in) Tj T*
(if true conception, attained consciousness. Here man, too, finds) Tj T*
(himself comprehended in his true nature, given in the specific) Tj T*
(conception of "the Son." Man, finite when regarded for himself, is yet) Tj T*
(at the same time the image of God and a fountain of infinity in himself.) Tj T*
(Consequently he has his true home in a super-sensuous world--an infinite) Tj T*
(subjectivity, gained only by a rupture with mere natural existence and) Tj T*
(volition. This is religious self-consciousness.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first abstract principles are won by the instrumentality of the) Tj T*
(Christian religion for the secular state. First, under Christianity) Tj T*
(slavery is impossible; for man as man--in the abstract essence of his) Tj T*
(nature--is contemplated in God; each unit of mankind is an object of the) Tj T*
(grace of God and of the divine purpose. Utterly excluding all) Tj T*
(speciality, therefore, man, in and for himself--in his simple quality of) Tj T*
(man--has infinite value; and this infinite value abolishes, _ipso) Tj T*
(facto_, all particularity attaching to birth or country.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The other, the second principle, regards the subjectivity of man in its) Tj T*
(bearing on chance. Humanity has this sphere of free spirituality in and) Tj T*
(for itself, and everything else must proceed from it. The place) Tj T*
(appropriated to the abode and presence of the Divine Spirit--the sphere) Tj T*
(in question--is spiritual subjectivity, and is constituted the place in) Tj T*
(which all contingency is amenable. It follows, thence, that what we) Tj T*
(observe among the Greeks as a form of customary morality cannot maintain) Tj T*
(its position in the Christian world. For that morality is spontaneous,) Tj T*
(unreflected wont; while the Christian principle is independent) Tj T*
(subjectivity--the soil on which grows the True.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Now, an unreflected morality cannot continue to hold its ground against) Tj T*
(the principle of subjective freedom. Now the principle of absolute) Tj T*
(freedom in God makes its appearance. Man no longer sustains the relation) Tj T*
(of dependence, but of love--in the consciousness that he is a partaker) Tj T*
(in the Divine existence.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_III.--The Germanic World_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The German world appears at this point of development--the fourth phase) Tj T*
(of world history. The old age of nature is weakness; but this of spirit) Tj T*
(is its perfect maturity and strength, in which it returns to unity with) Tj T*
(itself, but in its fully developed character as spirit.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
