(     philosophers at work trying either to refute what he had said or) Tj T*
(     continue lines that he had suggested, and out of them were created) Tj T*
(     both the Scotch and German schools of metaphysicians. Hume's) Tj T*
(     "Essays, Moral and Political," appeared in 1741-42, and followed) Tj T*
(     closely upon what he described as the "dead-born" "Treatise on) Tj T*
(     Human Nature," the success of the former going a long way towards) Tj T*
(     compensating him for the failure of the latter. In the) Tj T*
(     advertisement to a posthumous edition Hume complains that) Tj T*
(     controversialists had confined their attacks to the crude, earlier) Tj T*
(     treatise, and expressed the desire that for the future the "Essays") Tj T*
(     might alone be regarded as containing his philosophical sentiments) Tj T*
(     and principles. In the "Essays" Hume brings to bear the results of) Tj T*
(     his criticism upon the problems of current speculative discussion.) Tj T*
(     The argument against miracles is still often discussed; and the) Tj T*
(     work is well worthy of the author whom many regard as the greatest) Tj T*
(     thinker of his time. In 1751 he published his "Inquiry Into the) Tj T*
(     Principles of Morals," which is one of the clearest expositions of) Tj T*
(     the leading principles of what is termed the utilitarian system.) Tj T*
(     Hume died on August 25, 1776.) Tj T*
