(objects, we remember our perceptions, and this enables us to discern) Tj T*
(wherein things differ and wherein they agree. Then, by means of the art) Tj T*
(of induction, we arrive at ideas. Aristotle's theory of induction is) Tj T*
(clearly explained by him: "Experience furnishes the principles of every) Tj T*
(science. Thus astronomy is grounded on observation. For if we were to) Tj T*
(observe properly the phenomena of the heavens, we might demonstrate the) Tj T*
(laws which regulate them. The same applies to other sciences." Had he) Tj T*
