(_I.--Of Death, and How It Findeth a Man_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I was born between eleven of the clock and noon, the last of February,) Tj T*
(1533, according to our computation, the year beginning on January 1. It) Tj T*
(is but a fortnight since I was thirty-nine years old. I want at least as) Tj T*
(much more of life. If in the meantime I should trouble my thoughts with) Tj T*
(a matter so far from me as death, it were but folly. Of those renowned) Tj T*
(in life I will lay a wager I will find more that have died before they) Tj T*
(came to five-and-thirty years than after.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(How many means and ways has death to surprise us! Who would ever have) Tj T*
(imagined that a Duke of Brittany should have become stifled to death in) Tj T*
(a throng of people, as whilom was a neighbour of mine at Lyons when) Tj T*
(Pope Clement made his entrance there? Hast thou not seen one of our late) Tj T*
(kings slain in the midst of his sports? and one of his ancestors die) Tj T*
(miserably by the throw of a hog? Æschylus, fore-threatened by the fall) Tj T*
(of a house, when he was most on his guard, was struck dead by the fall) Tj T*
(of a tortoise-shell from the talons of a flying eagle. Another was) Tj T*
(choked by a grape-pip. An emperor died from the scratch of a comb,) Tj T*
(Æmilius Lepidus from hitting his foot against a door-sill, Anfidius from) Tj T*
(stumbling against the door as he was entering the council chamber. Caius) Tj T*
(Julius, a physician, while anointing a patient's eyes had his own closed) Tj T*
(by death. And if among these examples I may add one of a brother of) Tj T*
(mine, Captain St. Martin, playing at tennis, received a blow with a ball) Tj T*
(a little above the right ear, and without any appearance of bruise or) Tj T*
(hurt, never sitting or resting, died within six hours afterwards of an) Tj T*
(apoplexy. These so frequent and ordinary examples being ever before our) Tj T*
(eyes, why should it not continually seem to us that death is ever at) Tj T*
(hand ready to take us by the throat?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(What matter is it, will you say unto me, how and in what manner it is,) Tj T*
(so long as a man do not trouble and vex himself therewith? It sufficeth) Tj T*
(me to live at my ease, and the best recreation I can have that do I ever) Tj T*
(take. It is uncertain where death looks for us: let us look for her) Tj T*
(everywhere. The premeditation of death is a fore-thinking of liberty. He) Tj T*
(who has learned to die has unlearned to serve. There is no evil in life) Tj T*
(for him who has well conceived that the privation of life is no evil. I) Tj T*
(am now, by the mercy of God, in such a taking that, without regret or) Tj T*
