(     Aristocles, the son of Ariston, whose birth name is almost) Tj T*
(     forgotten because the whole world knows him as Plato, was born at) Tj T*
(     Athens about the year 427 B.C. As he grew up he became a devoted) Tj T*
(     disciple of Socrates, and when the Athenian people had put the) Tj T*
(     master to death, the disciple gave up his life to expounding the) Tj T*
(     wisdom of his teacher. How much of that teaching was really) Tj T*
(     implicitly contained in the doctrines of Socrates, it is difficult) Tj T*
(     to say, since very definite developments evidently took place in) Tj T*
(     Plato's own views. Plato himself lived to the age of eighty, and) Tj T*
(     died, as he had for the most part lived, at Athens, in 347. When) Tj T*
(     Socrates was indicted for "corrupting the youth" of Athens and on) Tj T*
(     other corresponding charges, Plato was himself present at the) Tj T*
(     trial. We may believe that the "Apology" is substantially a) Tj T*
(     reproduction of the actual defence made by Socrates. The "judges") Tj T*
(     in the Athenian court were practically the assembled body of free) Tj T*
(     Athenian citizens. When an adverse verdict was given, the accused) Tj T*
(     could propose a penalty as an alternative to that which had been) Tj T*
(     named by the accuser, and the court could choose between the two) Tj T*
(     penalties. Socrates was found guilty by a small majority of votes,) Tj T*
(     and sentence of death was passed, as set forth in the last section) Tj T*
(     of the "Apology.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_I.--The Official Indictment, and the Real Charges_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(What my accusers have said, Athenians, has been most specious, but none) Tj T*
(of it is true. The falsehood which most astonished me was that you must) Tj T*
(beware of being beguiled by my consummate eloquence; for I am not) Tj T*
(eloquent at all, unless speaking pure truth be eloquence. You will hear) Tj T*
(me speak with adornments and without premeditation in my everyday) Tj T*
(language, which many of you have heard. I am seventy years old, yet this) Tj T*
(is my first appearance in the courts, and I have no experience of) Tj T*
(forensic arts. All I ask is that you will take heed whether what I say) Tj T*
(be just.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is just that I should begin by defending myself against my accusers) Tj T*
(from of old, in priority to Anytus and these other latter-day accusers.) Tj T*
(For, skilful as these are, I fear those more--those who from your youth) Tj T*
(have been untruthfully warning you against one Socrates, a wise man, who) Tj T*
(speculates about everything in heaven and under the earth, and tries to) Tj T*
(make the worse cause the better. Their charge is the craftier, because) Tj T*
(you think that a man who does as they say has no thought for the gods. I) Tj T*
(cannot name these gentlemen precisely, beyond indicating that one is a) Tj T*
(writer of comedies; I cannot meet and refute them individually. However,) Tj T*
(I must try to enter a brief defence. I think I know where my difficulty) Tj T*
(will lie; but the issue will be as the gods choose.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Now, what is the basis of this charge, on which Meletus also relies?) Tj T*
("Socrates is an evil doer, a busybody, who pries into things in heaven) Tj T*
(and under the earth, and teaches these same things to others." You all) Tj T*
(saw the Socrates in the comedy of Aristophanes engaged in these) Tj T*
