() Tj T*
(_I.--How the Argument Arose_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I had gone with Glaucon to attend the celebration of the festival of) Tj T*
(Bendis--the Thracian Artemis--a picturesque affair, and we were just) Tj T*
(leaving, when Polemarchus insisted on carrying us off by main force to) Tj T*
(the house of his father, Cephalus. There we found a small company) Tj T*
(assembled. The old gentleman received us with hearty geniality; he is) Tj T*
(ageing, but would not see any hardship in that, if you take age) Tj T*
(good-humouredly. Of course, he owned that being wealthy makes a) Tj T*
(difference, but not all the difference. The best of wealth is that you) Tj T*
(need not do things which anger the gods and entail punishment in the) Tj T*
(hereafter; you need not lie, or be in debt to gods or men. And this) Tj T*
(consciousness of your own justice is a great consolation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("But," said I, "what is justice? Is it always to speak the truth, and) Tj T*
(always to let a man have his property? There are circumstances----") Tj T*
() Tj T*
("I must go," said he. "Polemarchus shall do the arguing.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This set us discussing the nature of justice. Glaucon took up the) Tj T*
(cudgels, after a preliminary skirmish with Thrasymachus.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Assuming justice to be desirable--is it so for itself and by itself, or) Tj T*
(only for its results; or both? The world at large puts it in the second) Tj T*
(category as an inconvenient necessity. To suffer injustice is an evil,) Tj T*
(and to protect themselves from that the weak combine to prevent) Tj T*
(injustice from being done. But if anyone had the ring of Gyges, which) Tj T*
(made him invisible, so that he could go his own way without let or) Tj T*
(hindrance, he would get all the pleasures he could out of life without) Tj T*
(troubling about the justice of it. Again, imagine on the one hand your) Tj T*
(really consummate rogue who gets credit for all the virtues and is) Tj T*
(surrounded by all the material factors of happiness; and, on the other) Tj T*
(hand, a man of utter rectitude, on whom circumstances combine to fix the) Tj T*
(stigma of iniquity. He will be rejected, scourged, crucified; while the) Tj T*
(other is enjoying wealth, honour, everything, and can afford to make his) Tj T*
(peace with the gods into the bargain.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Then Adeimantus took the field in support of his brother. "The poets,") Tj T*
(he said, "hold forth about the rewards of virtue here and hereafter. But) Tj T*
(we see the unrighteous prospering mightily; and the religious mendicants) Tj T*
(come to rich folks and offer to sell them indulgences on easy terms. A) Tj T*
(keen-witted lad is bound to argue that it is only the appearance of) Tj T*
