(qualified judge, places the satisfaction of the spirit second, and of) Tj T*
(desire lowest. And yet a third proof: I fancy the only quite real) Tj T*
(pleasures are those of the philosopher. There is an intermediate state) Tj T*
(between pleasure and pain. To pass into this from pleasure is painful,) Tj T*
(and from pain is pleasurable. Now, the pleasures of the body are really) Tj T*
(nothing more than reliefs from pains of one kind or another. And, next,) Tj T*
(the pleasures of the soul, being of the eternal order, are necessarily) Tj T*
(more real than those of the body, which are fleeting--in fact, mere) Tj T*
(shadows of pleasure.) Tj T*
