(_II.--On Kinds of Benefits and the Manner of Giving_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Let us give, in the first place, what is necessary; secondly, what is) Tj T*
(useful; next, what is pleasant, and one should add, what is likely to) Tj T*
(last. We must begin with what is necessary; for a matter involving life) Tj T*
(appeals to the mind differently from mere adornment and equipment.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A man may be a fastidious critic in the case of a thing which he can do) Tj T*
(without. But necessary things are those without which we cannot live, or) Tj T*
(without which we ought not to live, or without which we do not want to) Tj T*
(live. Examples of the first group are, to be rescued from the hands of) Tj T*
(the enemy, from a tyrant's anger, and the other chequered perils that) Tj T*
(beset human life. Whichsoever of these we avert, we shall earn gratitude) Tj T*
(proportionate to the terrible magnitude of the danger.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Next come things without which, it is true, we can live, yet only in) Tj T*
(such plight that death were better; such things are freedom, chastity,) Tj T*
(and good conscience. After these we shall rank things dear to us from) Tj T*
(association, blood-ties, use, and custom; such as children, wife, home,) Tj T*
(and all else round which affection has so entwined itself that it views) Tj T*
(severance from them as more serious than severance from life. There is) Tj T*
(the subsequent class of things useful, a wide and varied class,) Tj T*
(including money, not superabundant, but suited to a sensible mode of) Tj T*
(living; and public office, with advancement for those who look high.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Again, we ought to consider what gift will afford the greatest pleasure;) Tj T*
(and particularly ought we to take care not to send useless presents,) Tj T*
(such as weapons of the chase to a woman or an old man, or books to a) Tj T*
(block-head, or hunting nets to a person engrossed in literary pursuits.) Tj T*
(We shall be equally careful, on the other hand, while we wish to send) Tj T*
(what will please, not to insult friends in the matter of their) Tj T*
(individual failing; not to send wines to a toper, for instance, or drugs) Tj T*
(to a valetudinarian. Further, if free choice in giving lies in our) Tj T*
(power, we shall beyond everything select lasting gifts, in order that) Tj T*
(the present may be as little perishable as possible; for few are so) Tj T*
(grateful as to think of what they have received when they do not see it.) Tj T*
(Even the ungrateful have flashes of recollection when a gift is before) Tj T*
(their eyes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In a benefit there should be common sense. One should think of time,) Tj T*
(place, individuals; on these factors turn the welcome or unwelcome) Tj T*
(quality of gifts. How much more acceptable it is if we give what one) Tj T*
(does not possess, than if we give that of which he has abundance and to) Tj T*
(spare! Or the thing of which he has been long in quest without finding) Tj T*
(it, rather than what he is likely to see everywhere! A benefit bestowed) Tj T*
(upon all and sundry is acceptable to none. What you wish people to feel) Tj T*
(grateful for, do seldom. Let no one misconstrue this as an attempt to) Tj T*
(check generosity: by all means let her go any length she will; but she) Tj T*
(must go steady, not gad about.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
