(     Herbert Spencer was born at Derby, in England, in 1820. He was) Tj T*
(     taught by his father who was a teacher, and by his uncle, a) Tj T*
(     clergyman. At the age of seventeen he became a civil engineer, but) Tj T*
(     about eight years later abandoned the profession because he) Tj T*
(     believed it to be overcrowded. In 1848 he was engaged on the) Tj T*
(     "Economist," and five years later he began to write for the) Tj T*
(     quarterly reviews. Spencer's little book on Education dates from) Tj T*
(     1861, and has probably been more widely read than all his other) Tj T*
(     works put together, having been translated into almost all) Tj T*
(     civilised, and several primitive languages. It is generally) Tj T*
(     recognised as having effected the greatest educational reform of) Tj T*
(     the nineteenth century. It was certainly the most powerful of) Tj T*
(     single agents in effecting the liberation of girlhood from its) Tj T*
(     unnatural trammels. It placed the whole theory of education upon a) Tj T*
(     sound biological basis in the nature of the child and the natural) Tj T*
(     course of its evolution as a living creature. Spencer struck a) Tj T*
(     fatal blow at the morbid asceticism by proxy which adults used to) Tj T*
(     practice upon their children, and so great has been the influence) Tj T*
(     of his work for the amelioration of childhood that he is certainly) Tj T*
(     to be counted with the philanthropic on this ground. The first) Tj T*
