(_I.--What Knowledge is of Most Worth?_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It has been truly remarked that in order of time decoration precedes) Tj T*
(dress, the idea of ornament predominates over that of use. It is curious) Tj T*
(that the like relations hold with the mind. Among mental, as among) Tj T*
(bodily acquisitions, the ornamental comes before the useful. Alike in) Tj T*
(the Greek schools as in our own, this is the case. Men dress their) Tj T*
(children's minds as they do their bodies in the prevailing fashion; and) Tj T*
(in the treatment of both mind and body, the decorative element has) Tj T*
(continued to predominate in an even greater degree among women than) Tj T*
(among men. The births, deaths, and marriages of kings, and other like) Tj T*
(historic trivialities are committed to memory, not because of any direct) Tj T*
(benefit that can possibly result from knowing them, but because society) Tj T*
(considers them parts of a good education--because the absence of such) Tj T*
(knowledge may bring the contempt of others. Not what knowledge is of the) Tj T*
(most real worth is the consideration; but what will bring most applause,) Tj T*
(honour, respect--what will be the most imposing. As throughout life not) Tj T*
(what we are but what we shall be thought is the question, so in) Tj T*
(education the question is not the intrinsic value of knowledge so much) Tj T*
(as its extrinsic effect on others; and this being our dominant idea,) Tj T*
(direct utility is scarcely more regarded than by the barbarian when) Tj T*
(filing his teeth and staining his nails.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
