(     Jeremy Bentham, the son and grandson of attorneys, was born in) Tj T*
(     London on February 15, 1748. He was called to the Bar, but did not) Tj T*
(     practise. His fame rests on his work in the fields of) Tj T*
(     jurisprudence, political science, and ethics. He is accounted the) Tj T*
(     founder of the "utilitarian" school of philosophy, of which the) Tj T*
(     theory is that the production of the "greatest happiness of the) Tj T*
(     greatest number" is the criterion of morals and the aim of) Tj T*
(     politics. Dying on June 6, 1832, his body, in accordance with his) Tj T*
(     own wishes, was dissected, and his skeleton dressed in his) Tj T*
(     customary garb and preserved in the University College, London.) Tj T*
(     Bentham's failure at the Bar caused him no small disappointment,) Tj T*
(     and it was not until the publication of a "Fragment on Government") Tj T*
(     in 1776 that he felt himself redeemed with public opinion. The) Tj T*
(     "Principles of Morals and Legislation" was first published in 1789,) Tj T*
(     but was actually in print nine years earlier. It was primarily) Tj T*
(     intended as the introductory volume of a complete work designed to) Tj T*
(     cover the whole field of the principles of legislation--principles) Tj T*
(     which, as we have seen, were based on that doctrine of utility) Tj T*
(     which the author regarded as equally the basis of ethics.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_I.--Calculation of Pleasures and Pains_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Mankind is governed by pain and pleasure. Utility is that property in) Tj T*
(anything which tends to produce happiness in the party concerned,) Tj T*
(whether an individual or a community. The principle of utility makes) Tj T*
(utility the criterion for approval or disapproval of every kind of) Tj T*
(action. An act which conforms to this principle is one which ought to be) Tj T*
(done, or is not one which ought not to be done; is right, or, at least,) Tj T*
(not wrong. There is no other criterion possible which cannot ultimately) Tj T*
(be reduced to the personal sentiment of the individual.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The sources or sanctions of pleasure and pain are four--the physical, in) Tj T*
(the ordinary course of nature; political, officially imposed; moral or) Tj T*
