(Now we can see that the strength of a temptation equals the sum of the) Tj T*
(impelling motives, minus the sum of the tutelary motives. Hence, the) Tj T*
(more susceptible a man is to the standing tutelary motives, the less) Tj T*
(likely is he to yield to temptation; in other words, the less depraved) Tj T*
(is his disposition. Hence, given the strength of the temptation, the) Tj T*
(mischievousness of the disposition is as the apparent mischievousness of) Tj T*
(the act. Given the apparent mischievousness of the act, the less the) Tj T*
(temptation yielded to, the greater the depravity of disposition; but the) Tj T*
(stronger the temptation, the less conclusive is the evidence of) Tj T*
(depravity. It follows that the penalty should be increased--_i.e._, the) Tj T*
(fear of punishment should be artificially intensified, in proportion as,) Tj T*
(apart from that fear, the temptation is stronger.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We now come to consequences. The mischief of the act is the sum of its) Tj T*
(mischievous consequences, primary and secondary. The primary mischief) Tj T*
(subdivides into original, _i.e._, to the sufferer in the first instance;) Tj T*
(and derivative, to the definite persons who suffer as a direct) Tj T*
(consequence, whether through their interest, or merely through sympathy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The secondary mischiefs, affecting not specific persons but the) Tj T*
(community, are actual danger, or alarm--the apprehension of pain. For) Tj T*
(the occurrence of the act points to the possibility of its repetition;) Tj T*
(weakening the influence both of the political and of the moral sanction.) Tj T*
(An act of which the primary consequences are mischievous may have) Tj T*
(secondary beneficial consequences, which altogether outweigh the primary) Tj T*
(mischief--_e.g._, the legal punishment of crime. The circumstances) Tj T*
(influencing the secondary mischiefs of alarm and danger are the) Tj T*
(intentionality, the consciousness, the motive, and the disposition;) Tj T*
(danger depending on the real, and alarm on the apparent, state of mind,) Tj T*
(though the real and the apparent coincide more commonly than not.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Between the completely intentional and completely unintentional act) Tj T*
(there are various stages, depending on the degree of consciousness, as) Tj T*
(explained above. The excellence of the motive does not obliterate the) Tj T*
