(The metaphysical polity was required to combat the theological; but now) Tj T*
(it has served its destructive purpose, and tends to become obstructive,) Tj T*
(for, having destroyed the old, it will not permit the new. Its chief) Tj T*
(dogma has always been liberty of conscience with the liberty of press) Tj T*
(and speech which that implies; but liberty of conscience really means) Tj T*
(little more than absence of intellectual regulation; and even as liberty) Tj T*
(of conscience is out of the question in astronomy and chemistry, so it) Tj T*
(is out of the question in social physics. Liberty of conscience and) Tj T*
(inquiry can only be temporary and transitional, and must be followed by) Tj T*
(positive decision on the part of those qualified to decide. It cannot be) Tj T*
(held that every man is competent to form opinions in social and) Tj T*
(political questions; it cannot be maintained that intellects of weak) Tj T*
(capacity can judge obscure and complex questions, and that all opinions) Tj T*
(are equally valuable. All society is based on faith in the opinion of) Tj T*
(others and in reciprocal confidence. Continual discussion of the) Tj T*
(foundations of society must render it impossible to lay sure foundations) Tj T*
(firm, and the disorder produced by free opinions on all points by all) Tj T*
(people is seen in the fierce and feeble sectarianism of Protestantism.) Tj T*
(What are the limits of free inquiry we shall see later; meantime, we may) Tj T*
(note that fine motto of the Catholic Church: "In necessary things,) Tj T*
(unity; in doubtful things, liberty; and in all things, charity.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The second dogma of the metaphysical polity is equality, and, like the) Tj T*
(other dogma, it must be considered the temporary expression of a) Tj T*
(temporary need. It is indeed a corollary of the dogma of liberty of) Tj T*
(conscience; for to assume liberty of conscience without equality of) Tj T*
(intelligence would be to stultify the assumption. Having achieved its) Tj T*
(purpose, it also became an obstacle in the path of progress. Equality) Tj T*
(sufficient to permit a man to use his faculties aright is allowed by) Tj T*
(all; but men cannot be made equal physically, and much less can they be) Tj T*
(made equal intellectually and morally.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The dogma of liberty of conscience and equality resulted naturally in a) Tj T*
(third dogma, the sovereignty of the people. This also was provisionally) Tj T*
(useful, in that it permitted a series of political experiments; but it) Tj T*
(is in essence revolutionary, condemning the superior to be ruled by the) Tj T*
(inferior. A fourth dogma, the dogma of national independence, has also) Tj T*
(been serviceable in separating the nations in preparation for a new) Tj T*
(union.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The metaphysical polity fails utterly in constructive capacity. During) Tj T*
(the first French revolution it successfully destroyed the old social) Tj T*
(system; but its attempts to reorganise society were retrogressive.) Tj T*
(Instead of Catholicism it proposed polytheism; and in the name of virtue) Tj T*
(and simplicity it condemned industry and art. Even science was condemned) Tj T*
(as aristocracy of knowledge. Nor can these blunders be considered) Tj T*
(accidental; they were inherent in the polity. It is evident that a) Tj T*
(polity that admits on the one hand the need for a theological) Tj T*
(foundation, and on the other hand destroys the foundations of theology) Tj T*
(must end in intellectual anarchy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
