(Satisfied with neither the theological nor the metaphysical polities,) Tj T*
(society has wavered between them, and the one tendency has served) Tj T*
(chiefly to counteract the other. Out of these oscillations a third) Tj T*
(school of political opinion, which we may call the "stationary school,") Tj T*
(has arisen.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This school would fix society in a contradictory position between) Tj T*
(retrogression and progress, such as is seen in the parliamentary) Tj T*
(monarchy of England. This is a last phase of the metaphysical polity,) Tj T*
(and is only a kind of _placebo_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The result of all this is to produce a most unfortunate position. The) Tj T*
(theological polity would revert to old, worn-out principles; the) Tj T*
(metaphysical polity has no definite principles at all; and the) Tj T*
(stationary school merely offers temporary compromises. Everywhere there) Tj T*
(is intellectual anarchy, and in Protestant countries the disorder is) Tj T*
(increased by sectarian discord. So complex are all social questions that) Tj T*
(few are able to see them steadily, and see them whole, and where) Tj T*
(individual opinion is unhampered, individual prejudice and individual) Tj T*
(ignorance must be rampant.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Intellectual anarchy and unsettled convictions, moreover, tend to) Tj T*
(political corruption. If there are no convictions and no principles to) Tj T*
(which to appeal, appeal must be made to self-interest or to fear.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A growing tendency to take a shortsighted and material view of political) Tj T*
(questions is also a disturbing sign of the times. This is due to the) Tj T*
(fact that when, three centuries ago, spiritual power was abolished, all) Tj T*
(social questions were given over to men occupied with practical affairs) Tj T*
(and influenced chiefly by material considerations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Material views of political questions not only impede progress, but are) Tj T*
(also dangerous to order, for the view that disorders have a material) Tj T*
(cause leads to constant interference with institutions and with) Tj T*
(property. Granted there are abuses in connection both with property and) Tj T*
(institutions, what is required is not material changes but general moral) Tj T*
(and intellectual reform.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An inadequate and material view of social physics naturally favours) Tj T*
(mediocrity, attracts political charlatans, while the most eminent minds) Tj T*
(devote their attention to science.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The theological and metaphysical philosophies having failed, what) Tj T*
(remains? Nothing remains but the positive philosophy, which is the only) Tj T*
(agent able to reorganise society. The positive philosophy will regard) Tj T*
(social phenomena as it regards other phenomena, and will apply to the) Tj T*
(renovation of society the same scientific spirit found effective in) Tj T*
(other departments of human knowledge. It will bring to politics the) Tj T*
(conception of natural laws, and deal with delicate social questions on) Tj T*
(impartial scientific principles. It will show that certain wrongs are) Tj T*
(inevitable, and others curable; and that it is as foolish to try to cure) Tj T*
(the incurable in social as in biological and chemical matters. A spirit) Tj T*
