() Tj T*
(_III.--Social Statics_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There can be no doubt that society originated in social instincts, and) Tj T*
(was not merely the result of utilitarian considerations. Indeed, the) Tj T*
(social state could manifest its ability only when well developed, and in) Tj T*
(the early ages of humanity the advantages to the individual of) Tj T*
(association would not be obvious.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(What, then, are the human instincts and requirements which give society) Tj T*
(its fundamental characters? In the first place, it must be noted that in) Tj T*
(man the intellectual is subordinate to the affective. In most men the) Tj T*
(intellectual faculties are easily fatigued, and require a strong and) Tj T*
(constant stimulus to keep them at work. In the majority of cases the) Tj T*
(stimulus is derived from the needs of organic life; but in more highly) Tj T*
(endowed individuals the incitement may proceed from higher affective) Tj T*
(impulses. This subordination of the intellectual to the affective) Tj T*
(faculties is beneficent in that it gives a permanent end and aim to the) Tj T*
(intellectual activity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the second place it must be noted that the personal affections are) Tj T*
(stronger than the social affections, and that personal affections give) Tj T*
(aim and direction to our social actions. This is necessary, for all) Tj T*
(ideas of public good must be inferred from the ideas of private) Tj T*
(advantage, and if it were possible to repress our personal affections,) Tj T*
(our social affections, deprived of necessary inspiration and direction,) Tj T*
(would become vague and ineffective. In the precept that bids us love our) Tj T*
(neighbours as ourselves the personal instinct is suggested as the) Tj T*
(pattern for the social. The only thing to be regretted is that the) Tj T*
(personal affections are apt to override, instead of stimulating, the) Tj T*
(social affections.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Increase of intelligence must mean greater capacity for social) Tj T*
(affection, because of the discipline it imposes on the personal) Tj T*
(affections; and for the same reason increase of the social instinct is) Tj T*
