(poverty with progress is the great enigma of our time.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I propose to attempt to solve by the methods of political economy the) Tj T*
(great problem; to seek the law which associates poverty with progress) Tj T*
(and increases want with advancing wealth.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The inquiry is--why, in spite of increase in productive power, do wages) Tj T*
(tend to a minimum which will give but a bare living? The answer of) Tj T*
(current political economy is that wages are fixed by the ratio between) Tj T*
(the number of labourers and the amount of capital devoted to the) Tj T*
(employment of labour, and constantly tend to the lowest amount on which) Tj T*
(labourers will consent to live and reproduce; because the increase in) Tj T*
(the number of labourers tends naturally to follow and overtake any) Tj T*
(increase in capital. This argument is inconsistent with the general fact) Tj T*
(that wages and interest do not rise inversely, but conjointly. My) Tj T*
(proposition is that wages, instead of being drawn from capital, are in) Tj T*
(reality drawn from the product of the labour for which they are paid.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The three agents or factors in production are land, labour and capital,) Tj T*
(and that part of the produce which goes to the second of these factors) Tj T*
(is wages. Land embraces all natural materials, forces, and) Tj T*
(opportunities, and therefore nothing that is freely supplied by nature) Tj T*
(can be properly classed as capital. Labour includes all human exertion,) Tj T*
(and hence human powers, whether natural or acquired, can never be) Tj T*
(properly classed as capital.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We exclude from the category of capital everything which must be) Tj T*
(included either as land or labour, and therefore capital consists of) Tj T*
(those things which are neither land nor labour, but which have resulted) Tj T*
(from the union of these two original factors of production. Nothing can) Tj T*
(be capital which is not wealth; only such things can be wealth the) Tj T*
(production of which increases, the destruction of which decreases, the) Tj T*
(aggregate of wealth. Increase in land values does not represent any) Tj T*
(increase in the common wealth, for what landowners gain by higher prices) Tj T*
(the tenants or purchasers will lose.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(All wealth is not capital. Capital is only that part of wealth which is) Tj T*
(devoted to the aid of production. It is wealth in the course of) Tj T*
(exchange, for production includes not merely the making of things, but) Tj T*
(the bringing of them to the consumer. Wherever we analyse the facts we) Tj T*
(find that without production wages would not, and could not, be. As the) Tj T*
(rendering of labour precedes the payment of wages, and as the rendering) Tj T*
(of labour in production implies the creation of value, the employer) Tj T*
(receives value before he pays out value--he but exchanges capital of one) Tj T*
(form for capital of another form. Hence the payment of wages in) Tj T*
(production never involves the advance of capital or ever temporarily) Tj T*
(lessens capital.) Tj T*
