(The changes which constitute or contribute to material progress are) Tj T*
(three: increase in population, improvement in the arts of production and) Tj T*
(exchange, and improvement in knowledge, government, and morals. The) Tj T*
(effect of increase of population upon the distribution of wealth is to) Tj T*
(increase rent, and consequently to diminish the proportion of the) Tj T*
(produce which goes to capital and labour in two ways. First, by) Tj T*
(lowering the margin of cultivation; and second, and more important, by) Tj T*
(bringing out in land special capabilities otherwise latent, and by) Tj T*
(attaching special capabilities to particular land. The effect of) Tj T*
(inventions and improvements in the productive arts, including division) Tj T*
(of labour between individuals, is to save labour--that is, to enable the) Tj T*
(same result to be secured with less labour, or a greater result with the) Tj T*
(same labour, and hence to the production of wealth.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Without any increase in population, the progress of invention constantly) Tj T*
(tends to give a larger and larger proportion of the produce to the) Tj T*
(owners of land, and a smaller proportion to labour and capital; and,) Tj T*
(therefore, to decrease wages and interest. And, as we can assign no) Tj T*
(limit to the progress of invention, neither can we assign any limits to) Tj T*
(the increase of rent short of the whole produce. Another cause of the) Tj T*
(influence of material progress upon the distribution of wealth is the) Tj T*
(confident expectation of the future enhancement of land values which) Tj T*
(arises in all progressive countries from the steady increase of rent.) Tj T*
(This leads to speculation, or the holding of land for a higher price) Tj T*
(than it would otherwise bring. It is a force which constantly tends to) Tj T*
