(_IV.--The Remedy for Social Ills_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Private property in land is inconsistent with the best use of land. What) Tj T*
(is necessary for that is security for improvements. Where land is) Tj T*
(treated as public property it will be used and improved as soon as there) Tj T*
(is need for its use and improvement, but, being treated as private) Tj T*
(property, the individual owner is permitted to prevent others from) Tj T*
(using, or improving, what he cannot, or will not, use or improve) Tj T*
(himself. I do not propose to purchase or to confiscate private property) Tj T*
(in land. The first would be needless, the second unjust. It is only) Tj T*
(necessary to confiscate rent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The sovereign remedy which will raise wages, increase the earnings of) Tj T*
(capital, extirpate pauperism, abolish poverty, give remunerative) Tj T*
(employment to whoever wishes it, afford free scope to human powers,) Tj T*
(lessen crime, elevate morals and taste and intelligence, purify) Tj T*
(government, and carry civilisation to yet nobler heights, is to) Tj T*
(appropriate rent by taxation, and to abolish all taxation save that upon) Tj T*
(land values. The great class of taxes from which revenue may be derived) Tj T*
(without interference with production are those upon monopolies,) Tj T*
(temporary or onerous. But all other monopolies are trivial in extent as) Tj T*
(compared with the monopoly of land. Taxes on the value of land not only) Tj T*
(do not check production but tend to increase it by destroying) Tj T*
(speculative rent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The whole value of land may be taken in taxation, and the only effect) Tj T*
(will be to stimulate industry, to open new opportunities to capital, and) Tj T*
(to increase the production of wealth. A tax on land values does not add) Tj T*
(to prices, and is thus paid directly by the persons on whom it falls.) Tj T*
(Land is not a thing of human production, and taxes upon rent cannot) Tj T*
(check supply. On the contrary, by compelling those who hold land on) Tj T*
(speculation to sell or let for what they can get, a tax on land values) Tj T*
(tends to increase the competition between owners, and thus to reduce the) Tj T*
(price of land.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A tax on land values, while the least arbitrary of taxes, possesses in) Tj T*
(the highest degree the element of certainty. It may be assessed and) Tj T*
(collected with a definiteness that partakes of the immovable and) Tj T*
(unconcealable character of the land itself. It is the most just and) Tj T*
(equal of all taxes, because it falls only on those who receive from) Tj T*
(society a peculiar and valuable benefit, and upon them in proportion to) Tj T*
(the benefit they receive. The division of land now held on speculation) Tj T*
(would much increase the number of landowners. A single tax on the value) Tj T*
(of land would so equalise the distribution of wealth as to raise even) Tj T*
(the poorest above that abject poverty in which public considerations) Tj T*
(have no weight, while it would at the same time cut down those overgrown) Tj T*
(fortunes which raise their possessors above concern in government.) Tj T*
