(Principles of Political Economy) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     John Stuart Mill, the eldest son of the philosopher, James Mill,) Tj T*
(     was born in London on May 20, 1806. His early education was) Tj T*
(     remarkable. At the age of fourteen he had an extensive knowledge of) Tj T*
(     Greek, Latin, and mathematics, and had begun to study logic and) Tj T*
(     political economy. In 1823 he received an appointment at the India) Tj T*
(     Office, and in the same year he became a member of a small) Tj T*
(     Utilitarian society which met at Jeremy Bentham's house, and soon) Tj T*
(     became the leader of the Utilitarian school. Mill's great work on) Tj T*
(     the "Principles of Political Economy," with some of their) Tj T*
(     "Applications to Social Philosophy," embodies the results of many) Tj T*
(     years of study, disputation and thought. It is built upon) Tj T*
(     foundations laid by Ricardo and Malthus, and has itself formed the) Tj T*
(     basis of all subsequent work in England. Throughout, it manifests a) Tj T*
(     belief in the possibility of great social improvement to be) Tj T*
(     achieved upon individualistic lines. It was begun late in 1845, and) Tj T*
(     superseded a contemplated work to be called "Ethnology." Mill's) Tj T*
(     extensive familiarity with the problems of political economy) Tj T*
(     enabled him to compose the work with rapidity unusual in his) Tj T*
(     production. Thus, before the end of 1847, the last sheet of the) Tj T*
(     manuscript was in the hands of the printer, and early in the) Tj T*
(     following year the treatise was published. Mill died at Avignon on) Tj T*
(     May 8, 1873.) Tj T*
