(The Spirit of Laws) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     Charles Louis de Secondat, Baron de La Brede et de Montesquieu, was) Tj T*
(     born near Bordeaux, in France, Jan. 18, 1689. For ten years he was) Tj T*
(     president of the Bordeaux court of justice, but it was the) Tj T*
(     philosophy of laws that interested him rather than the) Tj T*
(     administration of them. He travelled over Europe and studied the) Tj T*
(     political systems of the various countries, and found at last in) Tj T*
(     England the form of free government which, it seemed to him, ought) Tj T*
(     to be introduced into France. For twenty years he worked at his) Tj T*
(     masterpiece, "The Spirit of Laws" \("De l'Esprit des Lois"\), which) Tj T*
(     was published anonymously in 1748, and in which he surveys every) Tj T*
(     political system, ancient and modern, and after examining their) Tj T*
(     principles and defects, proposes the English constitution as a) Tj T*
(     model for the universe. It may be doubted if any book has produced) Tj T*
(     such far-reaching effects. Not only did it help on the movement) Tj T*
(     that ended in the French Revolution, but it induced those nations) Tj T*
(     who sought for some mean between despotism and mob-rule to adopt) Tj T*
(     the English system of parliamentary government. "The Spirit of) Tj T*
(     Laws" is rather hard reading, but it still remains the finest and) Tj T*
(     the soundest introduction to the philosophical study of history.) Tj T*
(     Montesquieu died on February 10, 1755.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_I.--On a Republic_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There are three kinds of governments: the republican, the monarchical,) Tj T*
(and the despotic. Under a republic, the people, or a part of the people,) Tj T*
(has the sovereign power; under a monarchy, one man alone rules, but by) Tj T*
(fixed and established laws; under a despotism, a single man, without law) Tj T*
(or regulation, impels everything according to his will or his caprice.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When, in a republic, the whole people possesses sovereign power, it is a) Tj T*
(democracy. When this power is in the hands of only a part of the people) Tj T*
(it is an aristocracy. In a democracy the people is in certain respects) Tj T*
(the monarch, in others it is the subject. It cannot reign except by its) Tj T*
(votes, and the laws which establish the right of voting are thus) Tj T*
(fundamental in this form of government. A people possessing sovereign) Tj T*
(power ought to do itself everything that it can do well; what it cannot) Tj T*
(do well it must leave to its ministers. Its ministers, however, are not) Tj T*
(its own unless it nominates them; it is, therefore, a fundamental maxim) Tj T*
(of this government that the people should nominate its ministers. The) Tj T*
(people is admirably fitted to choose those whom it must entrust with) Tj T*
(some part of its authority. It knows very well that a man has often been) Tj T*
(to war, and that he has gained such and such victories, and it is) Tj T*
(therefore very capable of electing a general. It knows if a judge is) Tj T*
(hardworking and if the generality of suitors are content with his) Tj T*
(decisions, and it knows if he has not been condemned for corruption;) Tj T*
(this is sufficient to enable a people to elect its prætors.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(All these things are facts about which a people can learn more in a) Tj T*
(market-place than a monarch can in a palace. But does a people know how) Tj T*
