(     Thomas More was born in London on February 7, 1478; his father, Sir) Tj T*
(     John, was a magistrate. The boy was placed in the household of the) Tj T*
(     Chancellor, Cardinal Morton, and went to Oxford. The young man had) Tj T*
(     thoughts of entering the religious life, but finally chose the law.) Tj T*
(     His most intimate friend was the great Dean Colet, and his) Tj T*
(     relations with Erasmus, the chief of the Humanists, were of the) Tj T*
(     most affectionate kind. He stood with these two in the forefront of) Tj T*
(     the great effort for the intellectual and moral reform of the) Tj T*
(     Church, which was soon to be overwhelmed in the political and) Tj T*
(     theological Reformation. Drawn into public life by Henry VIII., he) Tj T*
(     became Chancellor after the fall of Wolsey, later resigned on a) Tj T*
(     point of conscience, and was finally beheaded on a charge of) Tj T*
(     treason on July 7, 1535, with Bishop Fisher, virtually for refusing) Tj T*
(     to acknowledge the secular supremacy over the Church. In 1886 he) Tj T*
(     was beatified. The "Utopia: Nowhere Land," was written in 1516, in) Tj T*
(     Latin. The English version is the rendering of Ralphe Robynson,) Tj T*
(     published in 1551. The three factors in its production were, the) Tj T*
(     discoveries in the New World, Plato's "Republic," and More's) Tj T*
(     observation of European affairs.) Tj T*
