(A few words will explain this: Natural rights are those which appertain) Tj T*
(to man in right of his existence. Of this kind are the rights of the) Tj T*
(mind, and also those rights of acting as an individual for his own) Tj T*
(happiness, which are not injurious to the natural rights of others.) Tj T*
(Civil rights are those which appertain to man in right of his being a) Tj T*
(member of society. Every civil right has for its foundation some natural) Tj T*
(right pre-existing in the individual, but to the enjoyment of which his) Tj T*
(individual power is not, in all cases, sufficiently competent. Of this) Tj T*
(kind are all those which relate to security and protection.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It follows, then, that the power produced from the aggregate of natural) Tj T*
(rights, imperfect in power in the individual, cannot be applied to) Tj T*
(invade the natural rights which are retained in the individual, and in) Tj T*
(which the power to execute is as perfect as the right itself.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Let us now apply these principles to governments. These may all be) Tj T*
(comprehended under three heads: First, superstition; secondly, power;) Tj T*
(thirdly, the common interest of society and the common rights of man.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When a set of artful men pretended to hold intercourse with the Deity,) Tj T*
(as familiarly as they now march up the back stairs in European courts,) Tj T*
(the world was completely under the government of superstition. This sort) Tj T*
(of government lasted as long as this sort of superstition lasted.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(After these, a race of conquerors arose, whose government, like that of) Tj T*
(William the Conqueror, was founded in power. Governments thus) Tj T*
(established last as long as the power to support them lasts; but, that) Tj T*
(they might avail themselves of every engine in their favour, they united) Tj T*
(fraud to force, and set up an idol which they called _Divine Right_, and) Tj T*
(which twisted itself afterwards into an idol of another shape, called) Tj T*
(_Church and State_. The key of St. Peter and the key of the treasury) Tj T*
(became quartered on one another, and the wondering cheated multitude) Tj T*
(worshipped the invention.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We have now to review the governments which arise out of society. If we) Tj T*
(trace government to its origin, we discover that governments must have) Tj T*
(arisen either _out_ of the people or over the people. In those which) Tj T*
(have arisen out of the people, the individuals themselves, each in his) Tj T*
(own personal and sovereign right, have entered into a compact with each) Tj T*
(other to produce a government; and this is the only mode in which) Tj T*
(governments have a right to arise.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This compact is the constitution, and a constitution is not a thing in) Tj T*
(name only, but in fact. Wherever it cannot be produced in a visible) Tj T*
(form, there is none. A constitution is a thing antecedent to government,) Tj T*
(and a government is only its creature. The constitution of a country is) Tj T*
(not the act of its government, but of the people constituting its) Tj T*
(government.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
