(The substitution of paper in the place of gold and silver money replaces) Tj T*
(a very expensive instrument of commerce by one much less costly, and) Tj T*
(sometimes equally convenient. Circulation comes to be carried on by a) Tj T*
(new wheel, which it costs less both to erect and to maintain than the) Tj T*
(old one. The effect of the issue of large quantities of bank-notes in) Tj T*
(any country is to send abroad the gold, which is no longer needed at) Tj T*
(home, in order that it may seek profitable employment. It is not sent) Tj T*
(abroad for nothing, but is exchanged for foreign goods of various kinds) Tj T*
(in such a way as to add to the revenue and profits of the country from) Tj T*
(which it is sent; unless, indeed, it is spent abroad on such goods as) Tj T*
(are likely to be consumed by idle people who produce nothing.) Tj T*
