(consists in the exchange of rude for manufactured produce, either) Tj T*
(immediately, or by the intervention of money, or of some sort of paper) Tj T*
(which represents money. The country supplies the town with the means of) Tj T*
(subsistence, and the materials for manufacture. The town repays this) Tj T*
(supply by sending back a part of the manufactured produce to the) Tj T*
(inhabitants of the country. The town, in which there neither is nor can) Tj T*
(be any reproduction of substances, may very properly be said to gain its) Tj T*
(whole subsistence from the country. And in how great a degree the) Tj T*
(country is benefited by the commerce of the town may be seen from a) Tj T*
(comparison of the cultivation of the lands in the neighbourhood of any) Tj T*
(considerable town with that of those which He at some distance from it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As subsistence is, in the nature of things, prior to conveniency and) Tj T*
(luxury, so the rural industries which procure the former must be prior) Tj T*
(to the urban industries which minister to the latter. The greater part) Tj T*
(of the capital of every growing society is therefore first directed to) Tj T*
(agriculture, afterwards to manufactures, and last of all to foreign) Tj T*
(commerce. But this natural order of things has, in all the modern states) Tj T*
(of Europe, been in many respects entirely inverted. The foreign commerce) Tj T*
(of some of their cities has given rise to their finer manufactures, and) Tj T*
(manufactures and foreign commerce together have given birth to the) Tj T*
(principal improvements of agriculture. The manners and customs which the) Tj T*
(nature of their original government introduced, and which remained after) Tj T*
(that government was greatly altered, necessarily forced them into this) Tj T*
(unnatural and retrograde order.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the ancient state of Europe, after the fall of the Roman Empire,) Tj T*
(agriculture was greatly discouraged by several causes. The rapine and) Tj T*
(violence which the barbarians exercised against the ancient inhabitants) Tj T*
(interrupted the commerce between the towns and the country; the towns) Tj T*
(were deserted and the country was left uncultivated. The western) Tj T*
(provinces of Europe sank into the lowest state of poverty, and the land,) Tj T*
(which was mostly uncultivated, was engrossed by a few great proprietors.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These lands might in the natural course of events have been soon divided) Tj T*
(again, and broken into small parcels by succession or by alienation; but) Tj T*
(the law of primogeniture hindered their division by succession, and the) Tj T*
(introduction of entails prevented their being divided by alienation.) Tj T*
(These hindrances to the division and consequently to the cultivation of) Tj T*
(the land were due to the fact that land was considered as the means not) Tj T*
(of subsistence merely, but of power and protection. In those disorderly) Tj T*
(times, every great landlord was a sort of petty prince.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Unfortunately these laws of primogeniture and entail have continued long) Tj T*
(after the circumstances which gave rise to them have disappeared.) Tj T*
(Unfortunately, because it seldom happens that a great landlord is a) Tj T*
(great improver. To improve land with profit requires an exact attention) Tj T*
(to small savings and small gains, of which a man born to a great fortune) Tj T*
(is seldom capable. And if little improvement was to be expected from the) Tj T*
(great proprietors, still less was to be hoped for from those who) Tj T*
(occupied the land under them. In the ancient state of Europe, the) Tj T*
(occupiers of land were all tenants at will, and practically slaves. To) Tj T*
