(these succeeded a kind of farmers known at present in France by the name) Tj T*
(of "metayers," whose produce was divided equally between the proprietor) Tj T*
(and the farmer, after setting aside what was judged necessary for) Tj T*
(keeping up the stock, which still belonged to the landlord. To these, in) Tj T*
(turn, succeeded, though by very slow degrees, farmers properly so) Tj T*
(called, who cultivated the land with their own stock, paying a fixed) Tj T*
(rent to the landlord, and enjoying a certain degree of security of) Tj T*
(tenure. And every improvement in the position of the actual cultivation) Tj T*
(of the soil is attended by a corresponding improvement of the land and) Tj T*
(of its cultivation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(After the fall of the Roman empire the inhabitants of cities and towns) Tj T*
(were not more favoured than those of the country. The towns were) Tj T*
(inhabited chiefly by tradesmen and mechanics, who were in those days of) Tj T*
(servile, or nearly servile condition. Yet the townsmen arrived at) Tj T*
(liberty and independence much earlier than the country population; their) Tj T*
(towns became "free burghs," and were erected into commonalities or) Tj T*
(corporations, with the privilege of having magistrates and a town) Tj T*
(council of their own, of making by-laws for their own government and of) Tj T*
(building walls for their own defence. Order and good government, and the) Tj T*
(liberty and security of individuals, were thus established in cities at) Tj T*
(a time when the occupiers of land in the country were exposed to every) Tj T*
(sort of violence.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The increase and riches of commercial and manufacturing towns) Tj T*
(thenceforward contributed to the improvement and cultivation of the) Tj T*
(countries to which they belonged, in three different ways. First, by) Tj T*
(affording a great and ready market for the rude produce of the country.) Tj T*
(Secondly, the wealth acquired by the inhabitants of cities was employed) Tj T*
(in purchasing uncultivated lands and in bringing them under cultivation;) Tj T*
(for merchants are ambitious of becoming country gentlemen, and when they) Tj T*
(do so, are generally the best of all improvers. And lastly, commerce and) Tj T*
(manufactures gradually introduced order and good government, and with) Tj T*
(them the liberty and security of individuals, among the inhabitants of) Tj T*
(the country.) Tj T*
