(_IV.--The Mercantile System_) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From the mistaken theory that wealth consists in money, or in gold and) Tj T*
(silver, there has arisen an erroneous and harmful system of political) Tj T*
(economy and of legislation in the supposed interests of manufacture, of) Tj T*
(commerce, and of the wealth of nations. A rich country is supposed to be) Tj T*
(a country abounding in money; and all the nations of Europe have) Tj T*
(consequently studied, though to little purpose, every possible means of) Tj T*
(accumulating gold and silver in their respective countries. For example,) Tj T*
(they have at times forbidden, or hindered by heavy duties, the export of) Tj T*
(these metals. But all these attempts are vain; for on the one hand, when) Tj T*
(the quantity of gold and silver imported into any country exceeds the) Tj T*
(effectual demand, no vigilance can prevent their exportation; and on the) Tj T*
(other hand, if gold and silver should fall short in a country, there are) Tj T*
(more expedients for supplying their place than that of any other) Tj T*
