(part of it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The principle of the "commercial system" or "mercantile system" is, that) Tj T*
(wealth consists in money, or in gold and silver. It is an utterly untrue) Tj T*
(principle. But once it had been established in general belief that) Tj T*
(wealth consists in gold and silver, and that these metals can be brought) Tj T*
(into a country which has no mines only by the "balance of trade," that) Tj T*
(is to say, by exporting to a greater value than it imports, it) Tj T*
(necessarily became the great object of political economy to diminish as) Tj T*
(much as possible the importation of foreign goods for home consumption,) Tj T*
(and to increase as much as possible the exportation of the produce of) Tj T*
(domestic industry. Its two great engines for enriching the country,) Tj T*
(therefore, were restraints upon importation and encouragements to) Tj T*
(exportation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The restraints upon importation were of two kinds: first, restraints) Tj T*
(upon the importation of such foreign goods for home consumption as could) Tj T*
(be produced at home, from whatever country they were imported; and) Tj T*
(secondly, restraints upon the importation of goods of almost all kinds) Tj T*
(from those particular countries with which the balance of trade was) Tj T*
(supposed to be disadvantageous. These different restraints consisted) Tj T*
(sometimes in high duties, and sometimes in absolute prohibitions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Exportation was encouraged sometimes by drawbacks, sometimes by) Tj T*
(bounties, sometimes by advantageous treaties of commerce with sovereign) Tj T*
(states, and sometimes by the establishment of colonies in distant) Tj T*
(countries. The above two restraints, and these four encouragements to) Tj T*
(exportation, constitute the six principal means by which the commercial) Tj T*
(or mercantile system proposes to increase the quantity of gold and) Tj T*
(silver in any country by turning the balance of trade in its favour.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The entire system, in all its developments, is a fallacious and an evil) Tj T*
(one. It is not difficult to determine who have been the contrivers of) Tj T*
(this whole mercantile system: not the consumers, whose interest has been) Tj T*
(entirely neglected; but the producers, and especially the merchants and) Tj T*
(manufacturers, whose interest has been so carefully attended to. It) Tj T*
(remains to be said, also, that the "agricultural system," which) Tj T*
(represents the produce of land as the sole source of the revenue and) Tj T*
(wealth of every country, and as therefore justifying a special) Tj T*
(protection of it, is as fallacious and as harmful as the other.) Tj T*
